#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143950} :activity/id #uuid "e3077b12-22c3-55b0-8214-f029f19bcdab" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049616 :topic/id #uuid "04c5db61-5d01-52c4-a151-d21a4cbe2bdb" :topic/math-academy-id 2382} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143950 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144078 :tutorial/content "To divide two fractions, we multiply the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following division problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\\div\\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nThe second fraction is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}5}\n$$\n\nand the reciprocal of this fraction is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 3 4 \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{\\color{red}5} = \\dfrac 3 4 \\times \\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 3 {4} \\times \\dfrac{5}{2} = \\dfrac {3 \\times 5} {4 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{15}{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\\div\\dfrac{2}{5} = \\dfrac{15}{8}\n$$\n\nSolving this problem using fraction models gives the same result:\n\n![](images/de/de4e554717f886b8e4b289a50d19e57f1b30383f0d7f4bf7b85476a57a442706.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "8a2b2b99-40dc-5215-997c-152bda4c83ae" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8601 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5d1d6077-d74e-529d-be1c-d9d7d7fbbd7e" :step/math-academy-id 97093 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143951}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143951 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142982 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142606 :question/id #uuid "7e77df17-125b-5a13-9429-dc1fdc15cb26" :question/math-academy-id "e-13780"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbee94b0-ad82-5461-81f0-84237abf0c85" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592